---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import StackDiagram from "../components/StackDiagram.astro";
import TinCard from "../components/TinCard.astro";
import PerfTable from "../components/PerfTable.astro";
import { tins, tinsByTier, TIER_LABEL } from "../data/tins";
import { perfGroups, passes } from "../data/perf";
import { upstreamIssues, STATUS_LABEL } from "../data/upstream";

const description =
  "An exploration of running data workflows on Mojo: a native Apache Iceberg stack — Parquet, Avro, Thrift, codecs, Roaring bitmaps, object storage and threads — published as tins on mojoshelf and gated against the reference implementation of every format.";

const worksToday = [
  "Iceberg format v1–v3 reads, with position deletes, equality deletes and v3 deletion vectors",
  "Schema evolution and nested types through the read path",
  "Fast-append, row-level delete both copy-on-write and merge-on-read",
  "Overwrite and dynamic partition overwrite",
  "expire_snapshots",
  "Filesystem and REST catalogs",
  "Local, HTTP, S3, GCS and Azure IO",
];

const notYet = [
  "Compaction — rewrite_manifests",
  "Encryption",
  "Brotli",
  "Predicates inside list and map elements",
];

---

<Base title="Data lake building blocks in Mojo" description={description} bare={false}>
  <Hero />

  <!-- ============================ what this is ============================ -->
  <section class="section section--alt" id="what">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this is</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">An experiment, not a product</h2>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Now that Mojo has reached 1.0 I used Claude to see if one can implement
            a meaningfully large data library in Mojo. I set my sights on
            <a href="https://iceberg.apache.org" rel="noopener">Apache Iceberg</a>.
            The following formats are implemented rather than wrapped: Parquet, Avro, Thrift,
            Iceberg itself, Roaring bitmaps, Snappy and the hashes all decode bytes in
            Mojo instead of delegating. Some very mature C libraries are currently
            linked, not reimplemented — <strong>libzstd</strong> and
            <strong>liblz4</strong> for those two codecs, <strong>libcurl</strong> for HTTP
            and TLS, and <strong>pthreads</strong> for the threads the language does not yet
            provide. What is gone is the Python and JVM layer that every other route from
            Mojo to Iceberg has to go through.
          </p>
          <p>
            What came out is the stack below. Some of the answers are encouraging — native
            Parquet decode is <em>faster</em> than pyarrow on a single core. Some are not:
            writes are slower, thread-pooled scans lose, and the language is still missing
            pieces a data stack assumes. I don't think the misses are limitations of the
            approach or the tech stack, it is a matter of putting in more time and tokens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="callout">
          <h3 class="callout__title">Packaged on mojoshelf</h3>
          <p>
            Magmalake leans on the packaging infrastructure defined by
            <a href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">mojoshelf.org</a> — a registry for
            Mojo <em>tins</em> on top of pixi. Every tin here is published there and installable with one
            command.
          </p>
          <pre class="callout__code"><code>pixi shelf add iceberg-mojo</code></pre>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            Working with a coding agent? <code>npx skills add mojoshelf/mojoshelf --skill
            mojoshelf-consume --yes</code> teaches it to find and install tins on its own —
            it installs the <code>shelf</code> CLI too.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            Tins are <strong>git source dependencies, not conda-channel packages</strong> —
            <code>pixi add</code> will not find them, which is the first thing people trip on.
            <code>pixi shelf add</code> resolves the tin and everything it depends on.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            Source tins can be consumed as git submodules, or as pixi git source dependencies.
            The pixi approach is recommended because it encapsulates any additional build process.
          </p>
          <a class="callout__link" href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">
            mojoshelf.org
            <svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"
                 stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M3 9 9 3M4.2 3H9v4.8" />
            </svg>
          </a>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================== the stack ============================= -->
  <section class="section" id="stack">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">The stack</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">How they layer</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        Iceberg sits on Parquet, Avro, Roaring and objectstore; those sit on Thrift, the
        compression codecs and the hashes; threads runs alongside all of it. I kept them
        separate, so you can take just the one you need.
      </p>
      <StackDiagram />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================ tins =============================== -->
  <section class="section section--alt" id="tins">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">The tins</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">What each one is, and what proves it</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Every tin is Apache-2.0, published on mojoshelf, and CI-tested on stable Mojo 1.0.0
        and the current nightly, across macOS and Linux. Each carries its own correctness
        oracle — an outside implementation I made it agree with.
      </p>

      {
        (["table", "format", "primitive", "oracle"] as const).map((tier) => (
          <div class="tier">
            <h3 class="tier__label">{TIER_LABEL[tier]}</h3>
            <div class:list={["tin-grid", tier === "table" && "tin-grid--lead"]}>
              {tinsByTier(tier).map((t) => (
                <TinCard tin={t} featured={tier === "table"} />
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================= performance ============================ -->
  <section class="section" id="performance">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Performance</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Measured, including where it loses</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        I measured all of this on 1 September 2026, on an Apple M4, single core unless a
        row says otherwise — same machine, same files, and every row reproducible from
        its own repository.
        magmalake beats pyarrow on single-core Parquet reads. It loses on writes and on
        multi-threaded scans, where what is left of the Iceberg gap is mostly libzstd
        itself.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        Every number here is a <em>mean over calibrated repetitions</em>, not a
        best-of-N, and each benchmark reports its own spread. That change alone moved
        several published figures — one benchmark had its random-number generator inside
        the timed loop, and most of the fast operations had been timing a cold pass over
        a buffer they had just built. Where a row measures a C library rather than Mojo,
        it says so.
      </p>

      <div class="perf-groups">
        {perfGroups.map((g) => <PerfTable group={g} />)}
      </div>

      <div class="panel" id="benchmarks" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 5rem;">
        <h3 class="panel__title">
          <a class="perf__anchor" href="#benchmarks">Benchmark history</a>
        </h3>
        <p>
          <strong>The table above is hand-measured; these are not.</strong> Those
          numbers were taken in one sitting on the M4 named at the top, which is why
          they carry a date rather than a build number. Every merge also runs the same
          benchmarks on a GitHub runner and appends to a history each repository
          publishes itself:
        </p>
        <ul class="bench-links">
          {
            tins.filter((t) => t.benchmarks).map((t) => (
              <li><a href={t.benchmarks} rel="noopener"><code>{t.name}</code></a></li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          Expect those to be slower and noisier than the table — a shared runner is not
          a workstation. They are worth reading as <em>trends</em>, not as absolute
          figures: each history is keyed by machine, so a run on a laptop and a run on
          CI stay separate series and are never averaged, and a benchmark is only
          flagged as regressed when it falls further than its own recent variance.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel" id="perf-reproduce" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 5rem;">
        <h3 class="panel__title">
          <a class="perf__anchor" href="#perf-reproduce">Reproducing any of this</a>
        </h3>
        <p>
          Every tin carries its own benchmarks and runs them through the same harness,
          <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/bench.mojo" rel="noopener">bench.mojo</a>.
          Clone the repo a row belongs to and run:
        </p>
        <pre class="callout__code"><code>git clone https://github.com/magmalake/parquet.mojo
cd parquet.mojo

pixi run -e bench bench                    # the table, with spread
pixi run -e bench bench -- --json          # every repetition, machine-readable
pixi run -e bench bench -- --list          # what this repo measures
pixi run -e bench bench -- --only bench_read_wide</code></pre>
        <p>
          The <code>bench</code> environment is stable Mojo 1.0.0: a precompiled Mojo
          package is stamped with the compiler version that built it, and every tin
          builds with 1.0.0. The libraries themselves test on stable and nightly.
        </p>
        <p>
          A few benchmarks need a fixture that is generated rather than committed —
          parquet wants <code>python tools/bench_pyarrow.py --make</code> first, iceberg
          builds a warehouse, objectstore needs MinIO. Each repo's README says which, and
          the bench stops with instructions rather than measuring the wrong thing. Expect
          your absolute numbers to differ from an M4; the ratios are what travel.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col two-col--wide">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3 class="panel__title">Threads, for now, come from a tin</h3>
          <p>
            Mojo's standard library currently has no threading. <code>parallelize</code> was
            removed and there is no thread pool reachable from user code, so every number
            above that mentions more than one core goes through
            <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/threads.mojo" rel="noopener">magmalake/threads</a>,
            which wraps pthreads directly via <code>external_call</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            That tin is <strong>inspired by and distilled from
            <a href="https://github.com/ehsanmok/flare" rel="noopener">flare</a></strong>
            — Ehsan Mokhtarian's project, MIT-licensed — which worked out how to get real OS
            threads out of Mojo in the first place. threads.mojo keeps the parts a data stack
            needs and drops the rest.
          </p>
          <p class="panel__fine">
            It is explicitly a stopgap. When the language ships its own threading, this tin
            should stop existing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="panel">
          <h3 class="panel__title">How these numbers were found</h3>
          <p class="panel__fine" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            Four optimisation passes. I profiled before each one instead of guessing, which
            was just as well — not one of the bottlenecks was where I would have looked first.
          </p>
          <ol class="passes">
            {
              passes.map((p) => (
                <li>
                  <span class="passes__found">{p.found}</span>
                  <span class="passes__fix">{p.fix}</span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================= correctness ============================ -->
  <section class="section section--alt" id="correctness">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Correctness</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Gated on someone else's implementation</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        A format library that only passes its own tests has proved nothing, so I gated every
        format tin here against the reference implementation of the format — in both
        directions where the format has two.
      </p>

      <div class="proofs">
        <div class="proof">
          <h3>Iceberg</h3>
          <p>
            Row-sets <strong>cell-exact against PyIceberg 0.11.1 and DuckDB 1.5.5</strong>
            across <strong>48 of 48 filter cases</strong>, in both directions, including
            deletes, deletion vectors and schema evolution. Tables magmalake writes are read
            back cell-exact by both — and PyIceberg can append to a table magmalake created.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof">
          <h3>Parquet</h3>
          <p>
            Value-exact against pyarrow on 33 fixtures — every value of every column — and
            pyarrow reads back every file magmalake writes. The Arrow export imports through
            <code>pyarrow.Array._import_from_c</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof">
          <h3>Avro &amp; Thrift</h3>
          <p>
            Avro round-trips against fastavro both ways across all four codecs. Thrift is
            <strong>byte-identical to Apache Thrift</strong> on generated wire vectors, in
            both the compact and the binary protocol.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof">
          <h3>S3 &amp; SigV4</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>37 of 37</strong> cases of the official AWS SigV4 test suite, every stage.
            S3 is verified end-to-end against MinIO <strong>in CI</strong>, not only on a
            developer's machine.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof">
          <h3>Roaring</h3>
          <p>
            Byte-exact against pyroaring in both directions, with pyroaring-produced payloads
            baked into the tests as constants so the oracle cannot drift.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof">
          <h3>Everything else</h3>
          <p>
            Codecs against Python <code>zstandard</code>, <code>lz4</code> and
            <code>python-snappy</code>; hashes against zlib, mmh3 and xxhash plus the Iceberg
            spec's own vectors; threads against contended-count and memory-visibility proofs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="upstream">
        <h3 class="upstream__title">Building it found bugs upstream</h3>
        <p class="upstream__blurb">
          Holding three implementations against each other surfaces disagreements, and not all
          of them turned out to be my fault. I cut each one below down to a standalone script
          or unit test with no magmalake code in it, then either reported it upstream or —
          where someone had got there first — matched it to the report that already existed.
        </p>
        <ul class="upstream__list">
          {
            upstreamIssues.map((u) => (
              <li>
                <a class="upstream__head" href={u.url} rel="noopener">
                  <span class="upstream__project">{u.project}</span>
                  <span class="upstream__ref">{u.ref}</span>
                  <span class:list={["upstream__tag", `upstream__tag--${u.status}`]}>
                    {STATUS_LABEL[u.status]}
                  </span>
                </a>
                <span class="upstream__bug">{u.bug}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =============================== status ============================== -->
  <section class="section" id="status">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Status</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Active, v0.x</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Reads are complete for Iceberg format v1 through v3. Writes cover the common table
        operations. Several things are genuinely missing, and they are listed here rather
        than left to be discovered.
      </p>

      <div class="status-grid">
        <div class="status-col status-col--yes">
          <h3 class="status-col__head">Works today</h3>
          <ul>{worksToday.map((w) => <li>{w}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="status-col status-col--no">
          <h3 class="status-col__head">Not yet</h3>
          <ul>{notYet.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
          <p class="status-col__fine">
            The spec's v4 drafts — relative paths and <code>content_stats</code> — are tracked
            but not implemented. What is proposed but unbuilt lives in
            <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/.github/issues" rel="noopener">the issue
            tracker</a> — currently
            <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/.github/issues/1" rel="noopener">a spec for a
            PostgreSQL tin</a>, which would let the SQL catalog run on something other than a
            local file.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cta">
        <div>
          <h3 class="cta__title">Take a tin</h3>
          <p class="cta__text">
            Each of the {tins.length} repositories is independently usable and independently
            installable. Start with whichever layer you need — you do not have to take the stack.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cta__actions">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://github.com/magmalake" rel="noopener">
            github.com/magmalake
          </a>
          <a class="btn" href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">mojoshelf.org</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* ---- shared section furniture ---- */
  .two-col {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.35fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .two-col--wide {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .two-col,
    .two-col--wide {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2.25rem;
    }
  }

  /* ---- mojoshelf callout ---- */
  .callout {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--lake);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.5rem 1.6rem;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  }
  .callout__title {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .callout p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .callout__code {
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }
  .callout__code code {
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    color: var(--lake);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }
  .callout__fine {
    font-size: 0.8125rem !important;
    color: var(--text-faint) !important;
  }
  .callout__link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .callout__link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  /* ---- tins ---- */
  .tier { margin-top: 2.75rem; }

  .tier__label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .tin-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 310px), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  /* iceberg is the only tin in its tier; give it the full width. */
  .tin-grid--lead { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* ---- performance ---- */
  .perf-groups { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  .panel {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.6rem 1.7rem;
  }
  .panel__title {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }
  .panel p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.62;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
  }
  .panel__fine {
    font-size: 0.8125rem !important;
    color: var(--text-faint) !important;
  }

  .passes {
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: pass;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.95rem;
  }
  .passes li {
    counter-increment: pass;
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 2rem;
  }
  .passes li::before {
    content: counter(pass);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.05em;
    width: 1.35rem;
    height: 1.35rem;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--ember-wash);
    color: var(--ember);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .passes__found {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .passes__fix {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
  }

  /* ---- correctness ---- */
  .proofs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 290px), 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .proof {
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 1.4rem 1.5rem;
  }
  .proof h3 {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--lake);
  }
  .proof p {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .proof strong { color: var(--text); }

  .upstream {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ember) 28%, var(--border));
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--ember-wash);
    padding: 1.6rem 1.7rem;
  }
  .upstream__title {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
  }
  .upstream__blurb {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }
  .upstream__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 240px), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .upstream__list li {
    border-left: 2px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ember) 45%, transparent);
    padding-left: 0.85rem;
  }
  .upstream__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 0.55rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .upstream__head:hover .upstream__ref { text-decoration: underline; }
  .upstream__project {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ember);
  }
  .upstream__ref {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .upstream__tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.625rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    color: var(--text-faint);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .upstream__tag--filed {
    color: var(--ember);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ember) 45%, transparent);
  }
  .upstream__bug {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ---- status ---- */
  .status-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .status-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  .status-col {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
